            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Judgment primarily refers to God's definitive eschatological evaluation of humanity, resulting in the separation of the righteous and the wicked, though it also includes historical punishments, fatherly discipline, vindication of the oppressed, and human moral discernment.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Judgment is fundamentally the act of governing, administering justice, and discerning right from wrong, as well as the resulting rulings; condemnation is only one aspect of it.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"God's righteous evaluation of human deeds, resulting in a just verdict (vindication or condemnation), primarily focused on the Last Judgment.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The act of governing, administering, and discerning right from wrong, including the rulings made.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_BROADENS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus heavily emphasizes the eschatological and punitive 'verdict' of God, whereas the book broadens the boundary to emphasize the ongoing administrative, governing, and justice-making functions of leadership, explicitly noting that condemnation is only a subset of judgment.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct material contradiction; the book shifts the center of gravity from the theological 'Last Judgment' to the broader biblical concept of justice and governance (reflecting the Hebrew concept of 'mishpat').\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book re-centers the definition away from a strictly eschatological or punitive framework, defining it broadly as the general act of governing and administering justice. The consensus recognizes elements of this in its alternatives (vindication, moral discernment), but the book makes it the primary definition.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"One might object that in the New Testament and historic Christian theology, 'Judgment' is so overwhelmingly identified with the eschatological Last Judgment that defining it primarily as general 'governing and administering' diminishes its dominant theological usage.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
